GCC 15.3.0, Darwin: give std::source_location objects linker-visible names

The C++ front end (cp/cp-gimplify.cc, fold_builtin_source_location) names
each __builtin_source_location() object with the prefix "Lsrc_loc" and emits
it in .data. On Darwin a capital-L label is assembler-local: no symbol is
produced, so under .subsections_via_symbols ld64 cannot see an atom boundary
and folds the object into the preceding atom. When that atom is a weak
definition (any libstdc++ template static, e.g. regex's _M_is_word::__s)
ld64 coalesces it with another TU's copy and the trailing bytes vanish;
every reference then resolves to the surviving atom + offset, i.e. into an
unrelated object's data. Measured 2026-11-02 on a C++ program: the
logger read function_name() as NULL and died in strlen. darwin.h already
does exactly this for the "LC" literal prefix (lC); this extends it.

--- a/gcc/config/darwin.h
+++ b/gcc/config/darwin.h
@@ -997,6 +997,17 @@
   do {							\
     if (strcmp ("LC", PREFIX) == 0)			\
       sprintf (LABEL, "*%s%ld", "lC", (long)(NUM));	\
+    else if (strcmp ("Lsrc_loc", PREFIX) == 0)		\
+      /* The C++ FE names each std::source_location	\
+	 object with this prefix and emits it as data.	\
+	 A capital L is assembler-local and leaves no	\
+	 symbol, so ld64's atom model folds the object	\
+	 into the preceding atom; when that atom is a	\
+	 coalesced weak definition the object is	\
+	 silently dropped and every reference lands	\
+	 in another TU's data (measured	\
+	 on 10.5).  'l' is linker-visible, as for lC.  */	\
+      sprintf (LABEL, "*%s%ld", "lsrc_loc", (long)(NUM));	\
     else if (strcmp ("Lubsan_data", PREFIX) == 0)	\
       sprintf (LABEL, "*%s%ld", "lubsan_data", (long)(NUM));\
     else if (strcmp ("Lubsan_type", PREFIX) == 0)	\
